{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at 7771921f026ac4ca78e0d34b13adf07196cfa966. All 23 artifacts reviewed. The skeleton and compile-only examples pass strict typechecking; the following protocol contradictions would propagate into every implementation lane. These are correctness and security corrections, not scope reductions.\n\nP1.1 — Make the signed log and publication construction acyclic (src/log.ts:119–145; src/policy.ts:221–225; R-ID-1, R-LANE-1, R-LOG-2/6/8/9, R-ADM-1 step 11). Checkpoint.commit names the Git commit whose tree contains that same signed checkpoint.json. The checkpoint bytes/signature determine the commit, so inserting that commit's SHA changes the commit being named. This cannot be built by ordinary content-addressed construction. Define a checkpoint over the prefix/hash independent of its containing commit, or publish an attestation in a later commit that names an earlier payload commit. Also settle entry self-references: a new claim's lane ID is derived from the entry hash but the opened receipt effect contains that lane ID; notify input contains the current ActId while the resulting decisions must be in that entry's hashed receipt. A post-commit notify cannot modify a sealed receipt, and a notify runtime failure cannot make an already-committed act unrecorded. Specify an explicit hash/ID/receipt order, self-reference representation or derivation, and separately recorded post-admission outcomes. Show a new claim plus notify and the first two log publications being constructed without a hash fixed-point or rewriting a signed entry. Align the types, log verification and policy inputs.\n\nP1.2 — The recovery boundary must cover the complete recovery path, before approval exists (R-ADMIN-3, R-ADM-1 step 9, R-OBL-5). The bypass applies to a configuration-only proposal only once obl_admin-approval is met. But propose is what creates the generation and obligation that a review can target. An active policy with refuse:true on propose prevents that generation from existing; refuse:true on review prevents its admin approval; refuse:true on claim can prevent acquiring a lane to start. Restoring an admin with the recovery key does not cure this policy lockout. Define a platform-authorized configuration-recovery path for the necessary claim/propose/admin-review/land steps, judged under current old-policy admin authority, while retaining signature/schema checks, actual .artroom-only changed paths, required admin approval, lease/generation fencing and the explicit sole-admin flag. Include a normative acceptance case starting with no held lane under a policy that refuses every ordinary workflow act. Admins must still be able to restore policy without applying the proposed policy to its own authorization.\n\nP1.3 — Split admission authority into direct member, delegation, join and recovery cases (R-ADM-3; R-GEN-3/4/6; R-CRED-3/4). R-ADM-3 unconditionally requires the signing key to belong to an active member whose own role allows the kind. A join's new key is not a member yet; the recovery key is explicitly not a member; an MCP session key is created and delegated without being joined; and the delegated Worker is supposed to act under the grantor's authority. The unconditional test rejects those documented flows or adds an unintended second membership/role gate to delegations. State which registry/authority checks apply to each case, including active grantor plus grant scope/expiry for a delegation, invitation validation for join and immutable recovery authorization. Clarify that the invitation binds the actor key at redemption, since invite has no public-key field, or add a pre-bound key if that is intended. The room-custody invitation also needs a typed redemption/bootstrap wire operation: neither RoomWire nor HttpRoutes presently offers a caller a way to obtain the first bearer without already having credentials or the private room-held key. Add compile-only onboarding examples for browser join, MCP redemption and an unjoined delegated Worker key.\n\nP1.4 — Define read authorization for capability-bearing workspace operations (landing.ts:39–54; transports.ts RoomWire.read, RoomApi.op/wait and GET ops; R-CRED-5/7/8, R-API-3). Workspace creation is holder-only, but WorkspaceOp.ready contains the fork write token and ordinary read/wait returns that whole object. The protocol gives active members read sessions without restricting reads of another holder's workspace operation. An implementation following the current read rules could turn a read credential into somebody else's write capability. Define a safe public operation view and a holder-only credential retrieval, or explicitly require current holder/lease and delegation authority for every token-bearing op read/wait. Cover revoked/removed callers and stale lease generations; secrets must not appear in attention, subscriptions, log, explanation, caches or errors. Add the acceptance case where member B reads/waits on A's workspace operation and receives no token.\n\nP2.1 — Restore the accepted test-input invariant (R-CARRY-3/8/9 and section 23). The immutable global-input list omits tests/**, although the approved plan's acceptance case expressly makes tests/** global. A scoped checker with inputs src/** can therefore omit a new failing tests/ file, keep an identical filtered snapshot and carry its pass. Whole-tree R-CARRY-6 protects only the default; it does not protect this scoped exception. Include tests/** and the promised test/build configuration in mandatory global inputs, or define an equally strong explicit alternative and revise the acceptance mapping. Include a scoped checker case with src unchanged and a new failing tests/ file: the check reruns on a snapshot that contains that file.\n\nArchitecture: the shared discriminated types, generation/lease fencing, historical evidence authority, immutable reservation, held uncertainty and recorded policy inputs are valuable and largely faithful to the approved plan. The five corrections above are needed before other lanes can safely treat this as normative. Continue the complete functionality; estimates do not decide approval.\n\nSecurity: the publisher/runner separation, current admission authority versus historical evidence validity, uncompromisable policy boundary and refusal-before-secret-publication rules are sound goals. The recovery, bootstrap/delegation and workspace-read gaps need precise rules now, rather than relying on each transport implementer to infer the same policy.\n\nSimplification: the types-only package and one shared RoomApi are a good boundary. Accept the documented additive preview-failed/unresolved states, terminal retryable operations, separate read sessions, roster-owned teams and provisional package scope. Runtime MCP descriptor generation in Lane E is reasonable provided the complete ten-tool wire shapes remain part of this contract; the boundary must preserve the same signed meaning and context on every transport.\n\nValidation: isolated detached worktree at the requested head; npm ci with scripts disabled; npm run typecheck passed including the full examples under strict/exact-optional/no-unchecked-indexed settings. Inspected every contract source, protocol rule, declared transport surface, example and configuration, and compared relevant invariants with the approved plan. No runtime implementation exists yet to execute the counterexamples; they are direct rule/type traces. No source changes or external mutations by checker.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:32e9165b4ed86a9c26e720607ecc2b83a1b44869","binding":"assigned","head":"7771921f026ac4ca78e0d34b13adf07196cfa966","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e764924cb419f99cc69b6805ed91e4dc4d1fe329\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a121cd85bf737bc30126316df28adf092ebacdc1\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4b2d845529104ac3d791e340e27731cf8201c584","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}